package com.acme.billing;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

@RestControllerAdvice(assignableTypes = InvoiceController.class)
public class BillingExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotFound(InvoiceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_FOUND", "Invoice not found"));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotCancellableException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotCancellable(InvoiceNotCancellableException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_CANCELLABLE",
                        "Only unpaid invoices can be cancelled"));
    }
}
```

**How refusals reach the client:**
- Someone else's invoice, or one that doesn't exist, gives `404 INVOICE_NOT_FOUND`. The two cases look the same, so ids can't be probed.
- Your own invoice that is no longer unpaid gives `409 INVOICE_NOT_CANCELLABLE`.
- Both are wrapped in `ApiResponse.error(...)`.

**Convention decisions applied:**
- The ownership and unpaid checks are in the service, not the controller, so they hold even if the route's security config changes. The controller makes one service call and wraps the result in `ApiResponse.ok`, with no try/catch.
- The response is a record in `InvoiceDtos`. Domain exceptions are mapped to `ApiResponse.error` by the advice. There is no Lombok, and no pagination because the endpoint returns a single item.

**Concurrency:** a payment landing at the same moment as a cancel could race. If that matters, add optimistic locking (`@Version`) on `Invoice` or a pessimistic lock in `findById`.
